<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<policyDefinitionResources xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/2006/07/PolicyDefinitions" revision="1.0" schemaVersion="1.0">
  <displayName/>
  <description/>
  <resources>
    <stringTable>
      <string id="CATEGORY_SimpleLAPSGUI">Simple LAPS GUI</string>
	  <string id="SUPPORTED_Version_2_5">At least Simple LAPS GUI 2.5</string>
	  
	  <string id="CopyLinkTargetsToClipboard">Copy links instead of opening them</string>
	  <string id="CopyLinkTargetsToClipboard_EXPLAIN">The app contains multiple web links. This policy helps you to control how they are handled.

If you enable this policy, all links are copied to the clipboard.

If you disable this policy, links are opened in the default browser.

If you do not configure this policy, the script parameter controls the behavior.
</string>
	  
	  <string id="WindowsEventLog">Enable logging to Windows Event Log</string>
	  <string id="WindowsEventLog_EXPLAIN">Configure the app to write a log into Windows Event Log

If you enable this policy, the app writes a log to Windows Event Log, and you can select the log level:
	- Error: Error events
	- Warning (default): Warning and error events
	- Information: Informational, warning and error events

If you disable this policy, no log is written.

If you do not configure this policy, the script parameter controls the behavior.

Note: To initialize this feature the app has to be installed using the setup.exe or you have to start the app once with administrative permissions.
</string>
	  <string id="WindowsEventLogLevel_ERROR">Error</string>
	  <string id="WindowsEventLogLevel_WARNING">Warning (default)</string>
	  <string id="WindowsEventLogLevel_INFO">Information</string>

	  <string id="LogFile">Log to file instead of Windows Eventlog</string>
	  <string id="LogFile_EXPLAIN">Configure the app to write a log into a files instead using Windows Eventlog

If you enable this policy, the app writes a log to the specified file instaed the Windows Eventlog.

If you disable this policy, Windows Eventlog is enforced as log target.

If you do not configure this policy, the script parameter controls the behavior.

Notes:
* The folder has to exist on the disk.
</string>
    </stringTable>

	<presentationTable>
	  <presentation id="WindowsEventLog">
        <dropdownList refId="WindowsEventLogLevel" noSort="true" defaultItem="1">Log level:</dropdownList>
      </presentation>
	  <presentation id="LogFile">
		<textBox refId="LogFilePath">
			<label>Log file path:</label>
		</textBox>
      </presentation>
	</presentationTable>
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>